-- facts/js-dataflow.rofl — the value core's vocabulary and its claims.
--
-- SEPARATE FROM facts/js-kinds.rofl ON PURPOSE, and the audit is what taught
-- me: the first draft put these `handled` rows in the base file, where the
-- kinds they name are not declared, and `orphan_claim[audit]` said so — a claim
-- about a cell that does not exist. Each layer's pack declares the vocabulary
-- it names, exactly as facts/js-callgraph.rofl does for the twenty-four kinds
-- the call graph dragged in. A claim and the kind it is about travel together
-- or neither is checkable.

node_kind(js, string_literal).
node_kind(js, numeric_literal).
node_kind(js, boolean_literal).
node_kind(js, identifier).
node_kind(js, object_expression).
node_kind(js, array_expression).
node_kind(js, function_expression).
node_kind(js, arrow_function_expression).
node_kind(js, class_declaration).
node_kind(js, parenthesized_expression).
node_kind(js, tsas_expression).
node_kind(js, tsnon_null_expression).
node_kind(js, object_method).
node_kind(js, class_method).
node_kind(js, optional_call_expression).
node_kind(js, return_statement).
node_kind(js, member_expression).
node_kind(js, optional_member_expression).
node_kind(js, object_property).
node_kind(js, this_expression).

-- THE VALUE CORE, 2026-09-04 (queue item w_df_value_core). Everything above
-- moves a value between names; these are the cells that say what a value IS.
-- `r_value_core` is rules/js-dataflow.rofl's may_be_lit / may_be_node.
--
-- FIVE OF THE EIGHT THE ITEM CLAIMED. `member_expression`, `object_property`
-- and `this_expression` are NOT ticked and are not oversights: reading a
-- property off a value is a second question (the call graph answers a narrow
-- case of it in `denotes`), and `this` outside a class method has no answer
-- this layer can give. They stay open with the default reason.
handled(js, string_literal,     dataflow, r_value_core).
handled(js, numeric_literal,    dataflow, r_value_core).
handled(js, identifier,         dataflow, r_value_core).
handled(js, object_expression,  dataflow, r_value_core).
handled(js, array_expression,   dataflow, r_value_core).

-- and the three transparent wrappers, which `w_df_control_forms` claimed and
-- this item closed by accident: a parenthesis, a cast and a non-null assertion
-- change nothing about a value, so they are one rule over a table rather than
-- three cells of work. The queue notices — a claim on a shut cell is a row.
handled(js, parenthesized_expression, dataflow, r_value_core).
handled(js, tsas_expression,          dataflow, r_value_core).
handled(js, tsnon_null_expression,    dataflow, r_value_core).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ACROSS A CALL (queue item w_df_function_forms). `r_call_flow` is the
-- argument-into-parameter and return-value half of rules/js-dataflow.rofl.
--
-- `new_expression` is NOT ticked: its arguments reach the constructor's
-- parameters like any other call, but the VALUE of `new C()` is an instance and
-- this layer has no notion of one. That is the same cell `w_cg_new_expression`
-- is about, seen from the other side.
handled(js, function_declaration,      dataflow, r_call_flow).
handled(js, function_expression,       dataflow, r_call_flow).
handled(js, arrow_function_expression, dataflow, r_call_flow).
handled(js, object_method,             dataflow, r_call_flow).
handled(js, class_method,              dataflow, r_call_flow).
handled(js, optional_call_expression,  dataflow, r_call_flow).
handled(js, return_statement,          dataflow, r_call_flow).

-- READING A PROPERTY OFF A VALUE, and `this` (queue item w_df_value_core,
-- finished). `r_member_value` is the member_value / selects / this half of
-- rules/js-dataflow.rofl. All three used to be answered privately inside the
-- call graph's `denotes`; they are dataflow facts and they came home.
handled(js, member_expression,          dataflow, r_member_value).
handled(js, optional_member_expression, dataflow, r_member_value).
handled(js, object_property,            dataflow, r_member_value).
handled(js, this_expression,            dataflow, r_member_value).

-- CONSTRUCTION (queue item w_df_function_forms, finished). `new C()` and the
-- name `C` both reach the class node; the instance/class conflation that buys
-- is declared in rules/js-dataflow.rofl and queued as w_df_instance_vs_class.
handled(js, new_expression, dataflow, r_construct).

-- BRANCHING VALUE FORMS, 2026-09-04. `a || b`, `a && b` and `a ?? b` are the
-- same may-set union as a conditional's two branches — measured, not assumed:
-- `(box || other).get()` resolved to nothing while `(flag ? box : other).get()`
-- resolved, on the same corpus in the same run, and both reported the SAME
-- callee shape. The two forms mean the same thing to a reader and one of them
-- was invisible.
--
-- The callgraph cell for this kind stays open on purpose: the value rule is
-- what is claimed here, and `(a || b)()` as a CALL callee has no site in the
-- corpus, so a callgraph tick would be a claim nothing could check.
handled(js, logical_expression, dataflow, r_value_logical).

-- THE CLASS AS A VALUE GREW THREE ANSWERS AND THE CELL STAYS OPEN, 2026-09-04.
-- The dataflow layer now says which methods a class has, which constructor it
-- answers with (its own or the nearest ancestor's), and which members it
-- inherits with a subclass's own declaration shadowing — the last of those
-- found by ADDING A FIXTURE rather than by reading, since the corpus had no
-- class extending another with a method to inherit.
--
-- AND NONE OF THAT TICKS THE CELL. `handled(js, class_declaration, dataflow,
-- r_class_chain)` was written here and `queue_stale[audit]` named it in one
-- run: `w_df_instance_vs_class` claims this very cell for a gap that is still
-- open — `new C()` and the name `C` both reach the class node, so an instance
-- is not told from its class. A kind-level cell at a layer with no refinement
-- column is ALL of that layer's answers about the kind, and three new ones do
-- not make it complete. The queue was the thing that knew.
